reviewer_comments.reviewer_4:
This experimental manuscript presents transport measurements of the longitudinal (ho_{xx}) and transverse (ho_{xy}) resistivities in underdoped, weakly overdoped, and heavily overdoped Bi-2201 cuprate superconductors. In addition to the “pure” superconducting state (ho_{xx}=0, ho_{xy}=0), the authors identify three additional states on the non-superconducting side as the magnetic field or temperature increases: (1) a vortexlike phase fluctuation state (ho_{xx} 
eq 0, ho_{xy}=0), (2) a Gaussian fluctuation state (ho_{xx} 
eq 0, ho_{xy} 
eq 0), and (3) a normal state, where ho_{xx} follows an H^2-dependent magnetoresistance. They emphasize that vortexlike phase fluctuations are pronounced in both the underdoped and heavily overdoped regimes but are significantly suppressed near optimal doping. Based on this, they conclude that the BCS mechanism is more applicable near optimal doping but fails to describe the underdoped and heavily overdoped cases. While the experimental results are intriguing, the presentation and interpretations are disorganized, and the underlying mechanisms are highly unclear. In particular, the claim and interpretation regarding the emergence of vortexlike phase fluctuations are not strongly supported by the experimental data. (1) Regarding the key results, why do the authors classify the state with ho_{xx} 
eq 0 and ho_{xy}=0 as a vortexlike phase fluctuation state? Can the authors provide experimental evidence demonstrating vortexlike features (short-range excitations) in this regime? (2) A nonzero Hall signal is typically associated with vortex generation due to magnetic fields or temperature, revealing singular superconducting fluctuations on the non-superconducting side [Nat. Phys. 20, 269 (2024)]. Clearly, the authors' interpretation of the ho_{xy} = 0 state as indicative of vortexlike fluctuations contradicts this understanding, and should address more discussion and evidence to support this interpretation. (3) The authors claim that ho_{xx} 
eq 0 and ho_{xy} = 0 indicate a nearly frozen or pinned vortex liquid. However, if vortices are pinned or frozen, leading to a vanishing ho_{xy}, it remains unclear why ho_{xx} remains nonzero. The origin of nonzero ho_{xx} in this scenario should be clarified. (4) There is inconsistency in how the phase fluctuation regime is described throughout the manuscript. The abstract, introduction, and discussion sections suggest that the ho_{xx} 
eq 0 and ho_{xy} = 0 state corresponds to the breaking of global phase coherence, while the main text associates it with vortexlike phase fluctuations and further classifies it as a frozen/pinned vortex liquid. These interpretations should be reconciled, as the breaking of global phase coherence is related to long-range phase fluctuations rather than short-range (vortexlike) ones. (5) "Our results strongly suggest that, in Bi-2201, strong vortex pinning that gives rise to R_H=0 with ho_{xx} is due to disorder". How can the authors arrive at this statement directly based on their experimental data? There is no disorder tuning in this experiment. (6) In the overdoped regime, as revealed by Ref.~12, short-range phase and amplitude fluctuations associated with disorder are expected to be strongly coupled as they should be according to the phase stiffness. How do the authors address this to explain the soley existing vortex phase fluctuations? (7) The statement, “Our results strongly suggest that it is thermal phase fluctuations that are predominantly responsible for the loss of phase coherence near the superconductor-to-metal transition in sufficiently disordered systems”, lacks clear justification from the presented data. How do the authors arrive at this conclusion to distinguish the role of quantum and thermal phase fluctuations? (8) The authors extract H_c' from ho_{xx}. Can a similar value be extracted from ho_{xy}, which is more directly related to vortex fluctuations? (9) Even near optimal doping, the Gaussian fluctuation regime remains significant. It is therefore misleading to claim that the optimal doping regime is reminiscent of a BCS-type superconductor, as BCS theory does not account for fluctuations, partly due to the Anderson-Higgs mechanism. (10) The figures in the manuscript are also poorly organized: The open black circles in Figs.~1c, 2c, and 3c do not aid in data interpretation. The open green circles in Fig.~1d do not provide meaningful information relevant to the manuscript’s conclusions. The results in Figs.~4a, 4b, and 4c are redundant with those in Fig.~4d. Given these concerns, I cannot recommend the manuscript for publication in its current form.
